 "597194": [
  {
   "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Entry points of other monosaccharides into glycolysis. Galactose is phosphorylated by galactokinase to galactose-1-phosphate, converted through UDP-galactose and UDP-glucose (using an epimerase) to glucose-1-phosphate, then by phosphoglucomutase to glucose-6-phosphate, where it joins glycolysis. Mannose is phosphorylated by hexokinase to mannose-6-phosphate and isomerized to fructose-6-phosphate. In muscle, fructose is phosphorylated by hexokinase to fructose-6-phosphate. In the liver, fructose is phosphorylated by fructokinase to fructose-1-phosphate, which aldolase B splits into dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde; glyceraldehyde is phosphorylated to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate. The liver route enters below phosphofructokinase-1, the main control point.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512608/fig-p597194-1.svg\" fileid=\"512608\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"Entry points of other monosaccharides into glycolysis. Galactose is phosphorylated by galactokinase to galactose-1-phosphate, converted through UDP-galactose and UDP-glucose (using an epimerase) to glucose-1-phosphate, then by phosphoglucomutase to glucose-6-phosphate, where it joins glycolysis. Mannose is phosphorylated by hexokinase to mannose-6-phosphate and isomerized to fructose-6-phosphate. In muscle, fructose is phosphorylated by hexokinase to fructose-6-phosphate. In the liver, fructose is phosphorylated by fructokinase to fructose-1-phosphate, which aldolase B splits into dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde; glyceraldehyde is phosphorylated to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate. The liver route enters below phosphofructokinase-1, the main control point.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597194-1-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597194-1-2.svg\" />",
   "count": 1,
   "note": "fig-p597194-1.svg as 2 print strips"
  },
  {
   "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"The pentose phosphate pathway. Oxidative phase: glucose-6-phosphate is oxidized by glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, reducing NADP+ to NADPH, to 6-phosphogluconate; a second dehydrogenase oxidizes and decarboxylates it to ribulose-5-phosphate, releasing carbon dioxide and a second NADPH. Non-oxidative phase: ribulose-5-phosphate is isomerized to ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis, or rearranged by transketolase and transaldolase into fructose-6-phosphate and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, which re-enter glycolysis. NADPH is used for fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis and to keep glutathione reduced in red blood cells.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512609/fig-p597194-2.svg\" fileid=\"512609\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"The pentose phosphate pathway. Oxidative phase: glucose-6-phosphate is oxidized by glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, reducing NADP+ to NADPH, to 6-phosphogluconate; a second dehydrogenase oxidizes and decarboxylates it to ribulose-5-phosphate, releasing carbon dioxide and a second NADPH. Non-oxidative phase: ribulose-5-phosphate is isomerized to ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis, or rearranged by transketolase and transaldolase into fructose-6-phosphate and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, which re-enter glycolysis. NADPH is used for fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis and to keep glutathione reduced in red blood cells.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597194-2-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597194-2-2.svg\" />",
   "count": 1,
   "note": "fig-p597194-2.svg as 2 print strips"
  }
 ],
 "597196": [
  {
   "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Glycogen metabolism map. Glycogenesis runs down the left: glucose is phosphorylated by hexokinase or glucokinase using ATP to glucose 6-phosphate, isomerized by phosphoglucomutase to glucose 1-phosphate, activated with UTP by UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase to UDP-glucose releasing pyrophosphate, and added to glycogen by glycogen synthase, which forms alpha-1,4 links and releases UDP; branching enzyme then makes alpha-1,6 branches. Glycogenolysis runs up the right: glycogen phosphorylase uses inorganic phosphate to release glucose 1-phosphate from the non-reducing ends, debranching enzyme releases about ten percent of the glucose as free glucose, phosphoglucomutase converts glucose 1-phosphate to glucose 6-phosphate, and in liver glucose-6-phosphatase hydrolyzes it to free glucose for the blood, while in muscle glucose 6-phosphate enters glycolysis.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512612/fig-p597196-1.svg\" fileid=\"512612\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"Glycogen metabolism map. Glycogenesis runs down the left: glucose is phosphorylated by hexokinase or glucokinase using ATP to glucose 6-phosphate, isomerized by phosphoglucomutase to glucose 1-phosphate, activated with UTP by UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase to UDP-glucose releasing pyrophosphate, and added to glycogen by glycogen synthase, which forms alpha-1,4 links and releases UDP; branching enzyme then makes alpha-1,6 branches. Glycogenolysis runs up the right: glycogen phosphorylase uses inorganic phosphate to release glucose 1-phosphate from the non-reducing ends, debranching enzyme releases about ten percent of the glucose as free glucose, phosphoglucomutase converts glucose 1-phosphate to glucose 6-phosphate, and in liver glucose-6-phosphatase hydrolyzes it to free glucose for the blood, while in muscle glucose 6-phosphate enters glycolysis.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597196-1-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597196-1-2.svg\" />",
   "count": 1,
   "note": "fig-p597196-1.svg as 2 print strips"
  },
  {
   "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Hormonal switch for glycogen metabolism. Glucagon acting on liver and epinephrine acting on liver and muscle raise cyclic AMP, which activates protein kinase A. Protein kinase A adds phosphate groups: phosphorylated glycogen phosphorylase is active and phosphorylated glycogen synthase is inactive, so glycogen is broken down. Insulin, released after a meal, activates a phosphatase that removes the phosphate groups: dephosphorylated synthase is active and dephosphorylated phosphorylase is inactive, so glycogen is built. In muscle, AMP also activates phosphorylase allosterically while ATP and glucose 6-phosphate inhibit it.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512613/fig-p597196-2.svg\" fileid=\"512613\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"Hormonal switch for glycogen metabolism. Glucagon acting on liver and epinephrine acting on liver and muscle raise cyclic AMP, which activates protein kinase A. Protein kinase A adds phosphate groups: phosphorylated glycogen phosphorylase is active and phosphorylated glycogen synthase is inactive, so glycogen is broken down. Insulin, released after a meal, activates a phosphatase that removes the phosphate groups: dephosphorylated synthase is active and dephosphorylated phosphorylase is inactive, so glycogen is built. In muscle, AMP also activates phosphorylase allosterically while ATP and glucose 6-phosphate inhibit it.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597196-2-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597196-2-2.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597196-2-3.svg\" />",
   "count": 1,
   "note": "fig-p597196-2.svg as 3 print strips"
  }
 ],
 "597197": [
  {
   "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Glycolysis and gluconeogenesis side by side. The shared intermediates run down the center: glucose, glucose 6-phosphate, fructose 6-phosphate, fructose 1,6-bisphosphate, the triose phosphates, 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate through 2-phosphoglycerate, phosphoenolpyruvate, and pyruvate. On the left, glycolysis runs downward; its three irreversible steps are hexokinase using ATP, phosphofructokinase-1 using ATP, and pyruvate kinase making ATP. On the right, gluconeogenesis runs upward using four bypass enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase in the mitochondrion uses ATP and biotin to make oxaloacetate, PEP carboxykinase uses GTP to make phosphoenolpyruvate, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase hydrolyzes a phosphate to give fructose 6-phosphate, and glucose-6-phosphatase in the liver hydrolyzes glucose 6-phosphate to free glucose. The seven reversible steps are shared by both pathways. Lactate, alanine, and glycerol enter on the right as gluconeogenic precursors.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512617/fig-p597197-4.svg\" fileid=\"512617\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"Glycolysis and gluconeogenesis side by side. The shared intermediates run down the center: glucose, glucose 6-phosphate, fructose 6-phosphate, fructose 1,6-bisphosphate, the triose phosphates, 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate through 2-phosphoglycerate, phosphoenolpyruvate, and pyruvate. On the left, glycolysis runs downward; its three irreversible steps are hexokinase using ATP, phosphofructokinase-1 using ATP, and pyruvate kinase making ATP. On the right, gluconeogenesis runs upward using four bypass enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase in the mitochondrion uses ATP and biotin to make oxaloacetate, PEP carboxykinase uses GTP to make phosphoenolpyruvate, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase hydrolyzes a phosphate to give fructose 6-phosphate, and glucose-6-phosphatase in the liver hydrolyzes glucose 6-phosphate to free glucose. The seven reversible steps are shared by both pathways. Lactate, alanine, and glycerol enter on the right as gluconeogenic precursors.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-2.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-3.svg\" />",
   "count": 1,
   "note": "fig-p597197-4.svg as 3 print strips"
  }
 ],
 "597202": [
  {
   "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"The citric acid cycle drawn as a ring of eight intermediates: oxaloacetate plus acetyl-CoA form citrate (citrate synthase), citrate isomerizes to isocitrate (aconitase), isocitrate is oxidatively decarboxylated to alpha-ketoglutarate (NADH and CO2), alpha-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA (NADH and CO2), succinyl-CoA to succinate with GTP formation, succinate to fumarate with FADH2, fumarate hydrated to malate, and malate oxidized to oxaloacetate with NADH. One turn yields 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, 1 GTP and 2 CO2.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512621/fig-p597202-1.svg\" fileid=\"512621\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"The citric acid cycle drawn as a ring of eight intermediates: oxaloacetate plus acetyl-CoA form citrate (citrate synthase), citrate isomerizes to isocitrate (aconitase), isocitrate is oxidatively decarboxylated to alpha-ketoglutarate (NADH and CO2), alpha-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA (NADH and CO2), succinyl-CoA to succinate with GTP formation, succinate to fumarate with FADH2, fumarate hydrated to malate, and malate oxidized to oxaloacetate with NADH. One turn yields 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, 1 GTP and 2 CO2.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597202-1-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597202-1-2.svg\" />",
   "count": 1,
   "note": "fig-p597202-1.svg as 2 print strips"
  }
 ],
 "597210": [
  {
   "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Hub diagram of how carbohydrate, lipid and protein metabolism overlap. Carbohydrates convert to and from pyruvate by glycolysis and gluconeogenesis; triacylglycerols give fatty acids and glycerol; proteins give amino acids. Pyruvate, fatty acids and ketogenic amino acids all feed acetyl-CoA, which enters the citric acid cycle or, in the liver, becomes ketone bodies, and which can be built back into fatty acids but not into glucose. Pyruvate and glucogenic amino acids feed oxaloacetate and alpha-ketoglutarate, citric acid cycle intermediates that can be turned into glucose. The cycle releases carbon dioxide and, through electron transport, ATP.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512629/fig-p597210-1.svg\" fileid=\"512629\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"Hub diagram of how carbohydrate, lipid and protein metabolism overlap. Carbohydrates convert to and from pyruvate by glycolysis and gluconeogenesis; triacylglycerols give fatty acids and glycerol; proteins give amino acids. Pyruvate, fatty acids and ketogenic amino acids all feed acetyl-CoA, which enters the citric acid cycle or, in the liver, becomes ketone bodies, and which can be built back into fatty acids but not into glucose. Pyruvate and glucogenic amino acids feed oxaloacetate and alpha-ketoglutarate, citric acid cycle intermediates that can be turned into glucose. The cycle releases carbon dioxide and, through electron transport, ATP.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597210-1-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597210-1-2.svg\" />",
