  }
 ],
 "597105": [
  {
   "old": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/pathways/oer/hydrogenation.svg\" alt=\"Partial hydrogenation of a vegetable oil. Top: a liquid triglyceride, a glycerol backbone with three ester-linked 18-carbon chains: two linoleate chains with two C=C double bonds each and one oleate chain with one C=C. Adding 3 H2 with a nickel catalyst and heat saturates some of the double bonds. Bottom: a partially hydrogenated, soft solid fat with two oleate chains and one fully saturated stearate chain. Fewer double bonds make straighter chains that pack together; partial hydrogenation can also convert some cis double bonds to trans.\" style=\"width:620px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
   "new": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/hydrogenation-1.svg\" alt=\"Partial hydrogenation of a vegetable oil. Top: a liquid triglyceride, a glycerol backbone with three ester-linked 18-carbon chains: two linoleate chains with two C=C double bonds each and one oleate chain with one C=C. Adding 3 H2 with a nickel catalyst and heat saturates some of the double bonds. Bottom: a partially hydrogenated, soft solid fat with two oleate chains and one fully saturated stearate chain. Fewer double bonds make straighter chains that pack together; partial hydrogenation can also convert some cis double bonds to trans.\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:620px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/hydrogenation-2.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:620px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/hydrogenation-3.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:620px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/hydrogenation-4.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:620px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
