  }
 ],
 "597203": [
  {
   "old": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/pathways/oer/respiration.svg\" alt=\"Overview of glucose catabolism. In the cytosol, glycolysis converts glucose (6 carbons) into 2 pyruvate (3 carbons each), with a net 2 ATP and 2 NADH. Without oxygen, pyruvate becomes 2 lactate in muscle or 2 ethanol and 2 CO2 in yeast, recycling NADH to NAD+, so only the 2 ATP from glycolysis are made. With oxygen, pyruvate enters the mitochondrion: 2 pyruvate become 2 acetyl CoA, 2 CO2 and 2 NADH; two turns of the citric acid cycle give 4 CO2, 6 NADH, 2 FADH2 and 2 GTP; electron transport passes electrons from NADH and FADH2 to O2, forming water, and ATP synthase makes most of the ATP, for up to about 32 ATP per glucose. All 6 carbons leave as 6 CO2.\" style=\"width:700px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
   "new": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/respiration-1.svg\" alt=\"Overview of glucose catabolism. In the cytosol, glycolysis converts glucose (6 carbons) into 2 pyruvate (3 carbons each), with a net 2 ATP and 2 NADH. Without oxygen, pyruvate becomes 2 lactate in muscle or 2 ethanol and 2 CO2 in yeast, recycling NADH to NAD+, so only the 2 ATP from glycolysis are made. With oxygen, pyruvate enters the mitochondrion: 2 pyruvate become 2 acetyl CoA, 2 CO2 and 2 NADH; two turns of the citric acid cycle give 4 CO2, 6 NADH, 2 FADH2 and 2 GTP; electron transport passes electrons from NADH and FADH2 to O2, forming water, and ATP synthase makes most of the ATP, for up to about 32 ATP per glucose. All 6 carbons leave as 6 CO2.\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:700px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/respiration-2.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:700px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/respiration-3.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:700px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
   "count": 1,
   "note": "respiration.svg as 3 print strips"
  }
 ],
 "597178": [
  {
   "old": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/pathways/oer/trna_loading.svg\" alt=\"Loading a tRNA in two steps, both catalyzed by aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase. Step 1: an amino acid (carboxylate, alpha carbon with NH3+ and H, side chain R) reacts with ATP; pyrophosphate is released and AMP is attached to the carboxyl group, giving aminoacyl-AMP. Step 2: the amino acid is transferred to the 3' OH of its tRNA as an ester, giving aminoacyl-tRNA (a charged tRNA), and AMP is released. Each amino acid has its own synthetase that matches it to the right tRNA.\" style=\"width:600px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
   "new": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/trna_loading-1.svg\" alt=\"Loading a tRNA in two steps, both catalyzed by aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase. Step 1: an amino acid (carboxylate, alpha carbon with NH3+ and H, side chain R) reacts with ATP; pyrophosphate is released and AMP is attached to the carboxyl group, giving aminoacyl-AMP. Step 2: the amino acid is transferred to the 3' OH of its tRNA as an ester, giving aminoacyl-tRNA (a charged tRNA), and AMP is released. Each amino acid has its own synthetase that matches it to the right tRNA.\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:600px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/trna_loading-2.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:600px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
